From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:01:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make the EMI pages about different things, instead of the
 same thing renamed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Measured before touching anything: comparing six-word runs of visible
text, the car and bike pages shared 64.1% of theirs. They were one page
with a word swapped — "car" for "bike", ₹5 lakh for ₹50,000 — down to an
identical FAQ question and eleven identical sentences of advice. That is
what Google's scaled content policy is aimed at, and no amount of
metadata fixes it.

Rather than merge six URLs into one, which would throw away five terms
people actually search for, each page now covers what is genuinely
different about its own loan:

- Bike: a small-ticket NBFC market, paperwork done at the dealership in
  an hour, zero-down offers, accessories folded into the on-road price,
  and a four-year tenure on an asset that will not be worth much by
  then. All fifteen answers rewritten.
- Car: secured but depreciating, so the tenure question is really about
  the gap between the balance and the resale value; dealer finance
  versus your own bank; hypothecation on the RC that has to be removed
  after the last EMI.
- Personal: unsecured, so the file is the security — income multiples,
  the fee taken out of the disbursal so you repay interest on money that
  never arrived, and how much more each extra year costs at these rates.

Every figure quoted is computed, not carried over: ₹80,000 at 11% over
one to four years on the bike page, ₹8,00,000 at 9.5% over three to
seven years on the car page, ₹5,00,000 at 12% and 16% on the personal
page.

Worst pair is now 31.6%, and what remains is form labels, section
headings and button text — the chrome a family of calculators is
supposed to share.

